          "Ultimately, institutionalizing robust tax controversy engineering safeguards corporate financial integrity, prevents unwarranted cash lockups in disputed demands, and defends enterprise shareholder value across all statutory and judicial forums.",
          "Read the scrutiny defense output as a two part test. First comes the jurisdiction check. The notice was served on 2026-06-20, and the limitation deadline is 2026-06-30. Ten days remained, so the lab prints jurisdictionally valid. Compare this with the example, where a notice dated July 10 is out of time against the same June 30 deadline, and a challenge based on Hotel Blue Moon is prepared.",
          "Second, the engine tests two proposed variations. Variation one is not quashable. Variation two is quashable, and the reason printed is a breach of the Section 144B requirement of a show cause notice. So a notice can be perfectly valid and still produce a flawed order. Jurisdiction and procedure are separate questions.",
          "The quiz states the rule. NaFAC cannot make an adverse addition without first serving an electronic show cause notice with a draft order. Without it, natural justice is violated and the order can be quashed. A wrong idea is that a valid notice protects every later step. It protects only the starting point of the case.",
          "Think of the scrutiny defense module as the front door of the whole project. Before anything else, the engine records the date on the notice, the date it was served, and the deadline that applies. Only after that does it look at what the officer actually proposed. This order matters, because a time barred notice makes every later argument unnecessary.",
          "Let us do the date check by hand, because the engine does exactly this. The financial year ends on March 31, 2026, and three months from then is June 30, 2026. In the lab, the notice was served on June 20, ten days before the deadline, so it is valid. In the example, the notice is dated July 10, which is ten days after the deadline, so it is late and the company can challenge the jurisdiction.",
          "The second test is about fairness. Variation one is not quashable. Variation two is quashable, because the faceless centre proposed an adverse addition without first serving the electronic show cause notice that the law requires. A student should note the pattern. The notice can be valid and the addition still be defective, because jurisdiction and procedure are separate checks."
