        "title": "Statutory Foundations & Taxable Event under Section 12: Territorial Waters & Maritime Zones Architecture",
        "say": [
          "The statutory architecture of Indian customs duty is anchored in Section 12 of the Customs Act, 1962, which serves as the supreme charging provision of the enactment. Under Section 12, duties of customs are levied at such rates as may be specified under the Customs Tariff Act, 1975, or any other law for the time being in force, on goods imported into, or exported from, India. The statutory interpretation of this concise charging section necessitates an exhaustive forensic examination of two fundamental statutory terms: goods and India. Under Section 2(22) of the Customs Act, goods are defined broadly to encompass vessels, aircraft, and vehicles, stores, baggage, currency and negotiable instruments, and any other kind of movable property. Intangibles such as software transmitted via physical media or imported engineering designs fall squarely within this movable ambit, as affirmed in landmark jurisprudence. The definition of India under Section 2(27) encompasses not merely the landmass of the subcontinent, but extends explicitly to the territorial waters of India. To establish the precise geographic perimeter where customs jurisdiction attaches, the Customs Act operates in tandem with the Territorial Waters, Continental Shelf, Exclusive Economic Zone and Other Maritime Zones Act, 1976.",
          "Under Section 3 of the Maritime Zones Act, 1976, the territorial waters of India extend to twelve nautical miles measured from the appropriate baseline along the coast. The contiguous zone extends to twenty-four nautical miles, within which customs authorities exercise security and sanitary jurisdiction. Beyond the territorial waters lies the Exclusive Economic Zone (EEZ), extending up to two hundred nautical miles from the baseline, wherein India possesses sovereign rights for the purpose of exploring, exploiting, conserving, and managing natural resources. By virtue of statutory notifications issued under Sections 6 and 7 of the Maritime Zones Act, the Central Government has explicitly extended the provisions of the Customs Act, 1962, and the Customs Tariff Act, 1975, to designated areas in the continental shelf and the EEZ, specifically covering offshore oil platforms, drilling rigs, and artificial islands such as Bombay High. Consequently, the supply of capital equipment, spares, and consumables to oil rigs situated in the EEZ constitutes an import if brought from foreign shores, or an export if dispatched from the domestic tariff area, subject to statutory reconciliations.",
          "The judicial determination of the exact taxable event in customs importation represents one of the most intellectually rigorous chapters in Indian fiscal jurisprudence. In the landmark Supreme Court decision of Garden Silk Mills Ltd. v. Union of India (1999), the apex court addressed the critical question of whether the taxable event occurs the moment a vessel enters the twelve nautical mile territorial waters of India, or when the goods are unloaded and brought to the customs barrier for clearance. The Supreme Court established the continuous taxable event doctrine: while importation commences when the carrying vessel enters the territorial waters of India, the taxable event is not completed at that exact geographical boundary. Importation is an ongoing, continuous process that commences upon crossing into territorial waters, but culminates only when the goods cross the customs barrier, clear customs control, and become commingled with the mass of goods in the domestic country.",
          "This continuous taxable event doctrine was further crystallized in the Constitutional Bench ruling in Kiran Spinning Mills v. Collector of Customs (1999). The Supreme Court held that for the purpose of determining the applicable rate of duty and tariff valuation, the taxable event is tied to the date of clearance of goods. Under Section 15 of the Customs Act, the rate of duty and tariff valuation applicable to any imported goods shall be the rate in force on the date on which a bill of entry in respect of such goods is presented under Section 46, or the date of entry inwards under Section 31, whichever is later. In the case of warehoused goods cleared under Section 68, the relevant date is the date of presentation of the ex-bond bill of entry for home consumption. Therefore, even if customs duty rates escalate or statutory exemptions expire between the date the vessel enters territorial waters and the date the bill of entry is presented, the importer is bound by the rate prevailing on the statutory critical date established under Section 15."
        ],
