        "title": "Duty Drawback Architecture: Section 74 Re-Exports vs Section 75 Manufacturing Remissions",
        "say": [
          "The statutory mechanism of Duty Drawback constitutes a cornerstone of India's export promotion architecture, codifying the internationally accepted economic doctrine that taxes and duties should not be exported. The Customs Act, 1962, provides two distinct statutory channels for granting drawback of customs duties: Section 74, governing drawback on the re-export of imported duty-paid goods, and Section 75, governing drawback on the export of manufactured goods utilizing imported or dutiable materials. Differentiating the operational scope, eligibility conditions, and calculation formulas between these two provisions is paramount for corporate indirect tax management.",
          "Section 74 provides drawback when goods that were previously imported into India, upon which customs duties were paid at the time of importation, are subsequently re-exported out of India. Under Section 74(1), where imported duty-paid goods are re-exported without being used in India, ninety-eight percent of the customs duty paid at importation is refunded as duty drawback. The remaining two percent is retained by the Central Government to cover port handling and administrative assessment costs. To qualify under Section 74(1), the goods must be conclusively identified by the proper officer as the identical goods imported, must be entered for export within two years from the date of payment of duty (extendable by the CBIC upon sufficient cause), and must clear customs under a Drawback Shipping Bill.",
          "Where imported goods are used in India prior to re-export, Section 74(2) empowers the Central Government to prescribe a sliding scale of reduced drawback percentages based on the duration of domestic use. Under Notification No. 19/65-Customs as amended, drawback is allowed at ninety-five percent if the period of use does not exceed three months; eighty-five percent for use between three and six months; seventy-five percent for use between six and nine months; seventy percent for use between nine and twelve months; sixty-five percent for use between twelve and fifteen months; and sixty percent for use between fifteen and eighteen months. If the period of domestic use exceeds eighteen months, the drawback entitlement drops to zero. Furthermore, for specific sensitive consumer commodities—such as motor cars, wearing apparel, and photographic films—separate depreciation schedules or complete exclusions apply.",
          "In contrast, Section 75 governs the refund of duties paid on imported materials used in the manufacture of goods that are exported. Under Section 75, the Central Government establishes All Industry Rates (AIR) of Duty Drawback, published annually in the Drawback Schedule. AIR rates represent average industry-wide rebate percentages determined on the basis of standard consumption norms and average customs duties borne by the export sector. Where an exporter establishes that the All Industry Rate covers less than eighty percent of the actual duties paid on inputs, the exporter can apply under Rule 6 or Rule 7 of the Customs and Central Excise Duties Drawback Rules, 1995, for the fixation of a Brand Rate or Special Brand Rate, backed by detailed engineering consumption data verified by the jurisdictional Commissioner.",
          "Where the Government delays the disbursement of duty drawback beyond one month from the date of filing the export claim, Section 75A(1) of the Customs Act mandates the payment of interest to the exporter at statutory rates (currently six percent per annum) from the expiration of that one-month period until the date of actual payment. Conversely, under Section 75A(2), if duty drawback has been erroneously paid or becomes recoverable due to non-realization of export proceeds within the FEMA timeline, the exporter must repay the drawback amount along with statutory interest at fifteen percent per annum under Section 28AA within two months of demand.",
          "The grant of duty drawback under both sections is circumscribed by the strict prohibitions codified in Section 76 of the Customs Act. Under Section 76(1)(a), no drawback shall be allowed on any goods the market price of which is less than the amount of drawback claimed thereon, preventing fraudulent over-invoicing of worthless scrap to extract treasury refunds. Under Section 76(1)(c), no drawback shall be allowed if the total drawback claim on any shipment is less than fifty rupees. In modern ICEGATE processing, duty drawback claims under Section 75 are processed automatically through electronic data matching between the Shipping Bill, the electronic Export General Manifest (EGM), and the exporter's verified bank account, providing rapid liquidity disbursement."
        ],
